39
00:03:52,814 --> 00:03:57,508
Now let's get started. So let's start with the basic understanding of what Agentic AI is.

40
00:03:57,966 --> 00:04:09,156
Agentic AI are goal-driven AI systems that can understand objectives, plan multiple-step action, and also autonomously use tools to achieve outcomes with limited human supervision.

41
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:11,268
Let's understand this step by step.

42
00:04:11,726 --> 00:04:13,732
First, goal-driven AI systems.

43
00:04:14,030 --> 00:04:16,320
Traditional AI systems are reactive.

44
00:04:16,526 --> 00:04:19,140
You give an input and they generate an output.

45
00:04:19,662 --> 00:04:21,600
But I dentic AI starts with a goal.

46
00:04:21,838 --> 00:04:27,588
Instead of simply responding, it asks what is the final outcome and also what do I need to achieve.

47
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:32,932
This shift from response based AI to outcome based AI is what makes it powerful.

48
00:04:33,326 --> 00:04:35,268
Second is to understand objectives.

49
00:04:35,726 --> 00:04:41,060
The system interprets what the user actually wants, not just the surface level instruction.

50
00:04:41,454 --> 00:04:43,812
It identifies the purpose behind the requests.

51
00:04:44,430 --> 00:04:50,116
For example, if objective is to increase sales, the agent doesn't just generate a report.

52
00:04:50,350 --> 00:04:53,796
It may analyze trends, identify gaps, and suggest strategies.

53
00:04:54,350 --> 00:04:56,708
Third is to plan multi-step actions.

54
00:04:57,230 --> 00:05:00,196
Real-world tasks are rarely one-step processes.

55
00:05:00,686 --> 00:05:07,364
They involve a sequence, gathering information, analyzing it, and also making decisions and also executing the tasks.

56
00:05:07,920 --> 00:05:11,428
Agentic AI creates this structured plan automatically.

57
00:05:11,886 --> 00:05:14,404
The next one is autonomously used tools.

58
00:05:14,958 --> 00:05:16,068
This is very important.

59
00:05:16,494 --> 00:05:19,268
The agent is not limited to generating text.

60
00:05:19,440 --> 00:05:25,572
It can call APIs, query data pieces, trigger workflows, and also interact with external systems.

61
00:05:26,062 --> 00:05:27,716
That's where real action happens.

62
00:05:28,302 --> 00:05:30,756
Finally, we have limited human supervision.

63
00:05:31,246 --> 00:05:34,244
Humans still define the goals and also guidelines.

64
00:05:34,640 --> 00:05:38,052
But within those boundaries, the agent works independently.

65
00:05:38,574 --> 00:05:47,204
So overall, Agentic AI transforms AI from a passive assistant into an active problem solver that works towards measurable outcomes.

66
00:05:47,694 --> 00:05:53,840
An agenticais system consists of three layers perception layer, cognition layer, and also action layer.

67
00:05:54,080 --> 00:05:56,420
Let's explore each layer in detail.

68
00:05:56,910 --> 00:05:58,020
The perception layer.

69
00:05:58,400 --> 00:06:02,628
This is a layer that ingests user inputs, events, and also data.

70
00:06:02,960 --> 00:06:06,800
This means it gathers all relevant information from the environment.

71
00:06:07,054 --> 00:06:12,420
It may receive user instructions, system notification, data streams, or even API responses.

72
00:06:12,974 --> 00:06:16,868
This layer ensures that the agent has situational awareness.

73
00:06:17,280 --> 00:06:21,892
Without accurate perception, decision would be based on incomplete information.

74
00:06:22,240 --> 00:06:24,100
Next, we have cognition layer.

75
00:06:24,494 --> 00:06:29,572
This layer plans, reasons, and also decides next actions using LN logic.

76
00:06:30,126 --> 00:06:31,908
This is where intelligence happens.

77
00:06:32,160 --> 00:06:39,236
The system analyzes the input, evaluates different options, and also predicts outcomes and selects the best courses of action.

78
00:06:39,520 --> 00:06:45,540
It combines reasoning, capabilities of large language models with logical rules and also memory systems.

79
00:06:46,158 --> 00:06:55,492
For example, if the goal is to schedule meetings, the cognition layer decides who to contact, check availability, and also prioritize based on importance.

80
00:06:56,046 --> 00:06:57,700
Then comes the action layer.

81
00:06:58,094 --> 00:07:02,276
This layer executes actions via tools, APIs, and also workflows.

82
00:07:02,766 --> 00:07:05,476
Once a decision is made, the agent performs the task.

83
00:07:05,742 --> 00:07:11,524
It may send emails, update records, call external services or trigger automated processes.

84
00:07:11,920 --> 00:07:13,956
So the full process becomes clear.

85
00:07:14,286 --> 00:07:18,852
First, ingest data, then reason about it, and finally execute the actions.

86
00:07:19,406 --> 00:07:24,420
This layered architecture makes Agentic AI structured, reliable, and also scalable.

87
00:07:24,814 --> 00:07:28,964
Now, let's compare single agent and also multi-agent systems.

88
00:07:29,440 --> 00:07:33,520
In single agent systems, one agent handles end-to-end flow.

89
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:40,484
This means it manages everything, understanding the goal, planning the steps, and also executing actions and monitoring outcomes.

90
00:07:41,070 --> 00:07:44,836
Single agent systems are simpler to design and also monitor.

91
00:07:45,102 --> 00:07:48,836
Since only one agent is involved, coordination is straightforward.

92
00:07:49,166 --> 00:07:56,004
This makes them ideal for narrow tasks such as report generation, email automation, or simple workflow management.

93
00:07:56,558 --> 00:08:01,924
They are the best for narrow tasks where complexity is limited and specialization is not required.

94
00:08:02,320 --> 00:08:06,916
On the other hand, multi agent systems involve specialized agents for subtasks.

95
00:08:07,200 --> 00:08:09,220
Each agent has a defined role.

96
00:08:09,520 --> 00:08:15,940
One might focus on planning, another on data gathering, another on validation, and another on execution.

97
00:08:16,334 --> 00:08:21,540
These agents work together using coordinator or protocols to sync their actions.

98
00:08:22,126 --> 00:08:26,436
Communication between agents ensures alignment towards overall goal.

99
00:08:26,894 --> 00:08:30,404
Multi-agent systems are best for complex cross-functional work.

100
00:08:30,958 --> 00:08:35,972
For example, in enterprise environments, different departments handle different responsibilities.

101
00:08:36,430 --> 00:08:40,100
Similarly, multi-agents collaborate to solve large problems.

102
00:08:40,366 --> 00:08:48,100
So the key difference is that single agents prioritize simplicity, whereas multi-agent systems prioritize scalability and specialization.

103
00:08:48,352 --> 00:08:53,604
The BIL loop stands for perceive, reason, act, and also learn.

104
00:08:54,126 --> 00:08:57,924
This represents the continuous operation cycle of an agentic AI system.

105
00:08:58,382 --> 00:08:59,652
First one is perceive.

106
00:08:59,872 --> 00:09:03,232
The agent reads the latest data and also events.

107
00:09:03,502 --> 00:09:06,948
It gathers information about the current situation before making decisions.

108
00:09:07,406 --> 00:09:11,140
This ensures that actions are based on real-time context.

109
00:09:11,854 --> 00:09:12,900
Second is reason.

110
00:09:13,358 --> 00:09:15,748
The agent chooses the best next step.

111
00:09:16,192 --> 00:09:22,916
It evaluates available options, considers constraints, and also locally determines what action should be taken.

112
00:09:23,374 --> 00:09:24,388
Third is ACT.

113
00:09:24,910 --> 00:09:28,804
The agent calls a tool or API to execute the decision.

114
00:09:29,262 --> 00:09:32,196
This step turns planning into measurable actions.

115
00:09:32,782 --> 00:09:35,312
Without this stage, reasoning would remain theoretical.

116
00:09:35,472 --> 00:09:36,612
The fourth one is learn.

117
00:09:37,134 --> 00:09:39,312
The agent updates memory and strategy.

118
00:09:39,534 --> 00:09:43,780
It evaluates the outcomes of its actions and refines further behavior.

119
00:09:44,174 --> 00:09:47,108
This makes the systems adaptive rather than static.

120
00:09:47,566 --> 00:09:49,508
The PRAL loop is continuous.

121
00:09:49,902 --> 00:09:56,004
After learning, the agent perceives again, reasons again, acts again, and also learn again.

122
00:09:56,462 --> 00:09:58,884
This cycle enables continuous improvement.

123
00:09:59,246 --> 00:10:04,592
This loop is what makes agentic AI dynamic and also intelligent rather than just automated.

124
00:10:05,070 --> 00:10:08,484
Next, here we have three common agentic design patterns.

125
00:10:08,672 --> 00:10:10,884
The first one is planner-executor.

126
00:10:11,374 --> 00:10:15,300
This pattern separates long-term planning from low-key actions.

127
00:10:15,918 --> 00:10:21,668
One component focuses on strategy and decision making while other handles execution.

128
00:10:22,126 --> 00:10:25,220
This separates increases clarity and reduces errors.

129
00:10:25,774 --> 00:10:27,472
Second is tool user.

130
00:10:27,632 --> 00:10:32,516
In this pattern, the agent selects the invokes external tools based on a goal.

131
00:10:32,878 --> 00:10:40,612
Instead of relying only on internal reasoning, it leverages APIs, databases, search engines, or even computational tools.

132
00:10:41,198 --> 00:10:43,952
Choosing the right tool at the right time is critical.

133
00:10:44,206 --> 00:10:45,828
Third is reflective agent.

134
00:10:46,192 --> 00:10:54,404
This type of agent self-evaluates its own outputs and improves them. After generating a result, it reviews and refines it.

135
00:10:54,894 --> 00:10:57,604
This improves reliability and also quality.

136
00:10:58,062 --> 00:10:59,872
Three patterns provide structure.

137
00:11:00,032 --> 00:11:09,828
Instead of building random automation workflows, developers can follow proven architecture approaches to design scalable and also efficient agentic AI systems.

138
00:11:10,382 --> 00:11:15,108
Here we will see relational between risk of impact and level of autonomy.

139
00:11:16,398 --> 00:11:21,796
As you know, when risk is low, safe automation such as suggestion can only be allowed.

140
00:11:22,222 --> 00:11:25,924
For example, content recommendation or draft emails.

141
00:11:26,352 --> 00:11:30,724
As autonomy increases, we may allow batch actions with review.

142
00:11:31,086 --> 00:11:36,228
This means the agent performs tasks, but humans approve them before final execution.

143
00:11:36,654 --> 00:11:42,756
When systems access sensitive environments, limited access is important to reduce risks.

144
00:11:43,406 --> 00:11:48,164
For high-stakes agents, strict guardrails and also approvals are necessary.

145
00:11:48,526 --> 00:11:52,164
These systems require strong monitoring and also human oversight.

146
00:11:52,512 --> 00:11:59,152
The key takeaway here is to increase autonomy slowly as guardrails and monitoring mature.

147
00:11:59,598 --> 00:12:04,292
We should never jump directly into fully autonomy in high-risk environments.

148
00:12:04,814 --> 00:12:11,588
Balancing autonomy and control ensures innovation without compromising safety and also accountability.

